{
 "source": "LDP LLC (MaxMax.com), \"Spectral Response\" (historical page, last modified 18 June 2015, https://www.maxmax.com/spectral_response.htm): adjusted RGB response curves of converted cameras measured with a monochromator, 375-1000nm, no lens; the camera output corrected against a spectrometer reading of the same monochromator light.",
 "method": "Traced programmatically from each published chart (about 710x455 px): the wavelength axis calibrated from the chart's own vertical gridlines (350-950nm every 100nm, 1000nm at the plot edge), the zero line from its bottom gridline; each channel isolated by line colour (R, G, B), the median row of that colour taken in every pixel column below the strip of sample photos MaxMax drew across the top, and columns where another line hides it interpolated. Read every 10nm from 380 to 1000nm, in pixels above the zero line (the vertical scale does not matter here: irlab white-balances each channel).",
 "caveats": "MaxMax compiled the values in Photoshop; if the files were not linear the curve shapes carry some bend. On UltravioletPhotography.com Andy Perrin and others have questioned the channel levels of these old plots above 850nm (the channels should converge there); the levels do not matter for irlab's fit, the shapes do. Independent check (build 249): direct raw measurements of other converted cameras find all three channels equally sensitive from about 840nm (Crowther 2022, J. Imaging 8:54, Sony A7 III; Aphalo 2024, photo-spectrum.info, full-spectrum Olympus E-M1, CC BY-SA 4.0); these curves do not converge there, which suggests the values were read after white balance or a colour conversion. Kept as options with that warning; not the default.",
